  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Confirm from live metadata which entities carry `sprk_issecure`, and confirm no server path currently reads `sprk_project.sprk_containerid` outside provisioning.</step>
    <step order="1">Design the seam: signature, where it lives, how client and server halves stay consistent under INV-7, and how the reverse lookup is exposed for task 073. Write this down before coding.</step>
    <step order="2">Implement, with fail-closed as the default branch for secure records rather than an afterthought.</step>
    <step order="3">Give strategy 2 (server-side ingest / `ArchiveContainerId`) its equivalent decision — it has no client and is the one most likely to be missed.</step>
    <step order="4">Tests that FAIL without the fix: a secure record resolves to its own container; a secure record with a null container FAILS rather than falling back; a non-secure record still resolves via the BU cascade. Include the perturbation check — break the secure branch and confirm a test goes red.</step>
    <step order="5">Build, tests, publish size, CVE. Record the seam design and the drift-prevention argument in notes.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If INV-7 makes a single shared decision genuinely impossible and two implementations are unavoidable, STOP and surface it. Two implementations of an isolation rule is a design decision with a known failure mode, not an implementation detail to absorb quietly.</trigger>
    <trigger>If existing documents are found already sitting in a shared container for a record that is (or becomes) secure, STOP — that is a migration, and this task does not own it. Note that dev currently has ZERO secure projects, so this should not arise; if it does, the assumption behind the whole plan is wrong.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>Provisioning already creates and stamps the per-project container (task 021). The BU cascade resolver already exists client-side. `SpeFileStore` already brokers SPE access.</existing>
    <extension>Cannot extend an existing resolver: the BU cascade is client-side by design (INV-7) and has no notion of record security, while the archive path is server-side config with no resolver at all. This component is the join between them, and it is the only new surface Wave 2 adds.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Provisioning writes a container id that nothing reads, so every secure document lands in a shared container. Because SPE permissions are additive-only, no later per-item permission can undo that — the content is readable by every member of the shared container, outside the BFF's control entirely.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">Record-aware container resolver (shared seam, bidirectional)</output>
    <output type="code">Server-side equivalent for the ingest/archive path</output>
    <output type="test">tests/ — secure→own container, secure+null→fail closed, non-secure→BU cascade</output>
    <output type="doc">notes — seam design, INV-7 split, drift prevention</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>A secure record resolves to its own `sprk_containerid`, proven by a test that fails without the change.</criterion>
    <criterion>A secure record with a null container FAILS CLOSED — no fallback to BU or archive — proven by a test.</criterion>
    <criterion>A non-secure record still resolves through the BU cascade unchanged.</criterion>
    <criterion>The securable-entity list is derived from live metadata, not hard-coded.</criterion>
    <criterion>The reverse mapping (container → owning record) is exposed and consumed by task 073 — exactly one mapping in the codebase.</criterion>
    <criterion>The server-side ingest/archive path uses the same decision.</criterion>
    <criterion>Perturbation check performed: breaking the secure branch turns a test red.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Task 076 routes the call sites; this task builds the thing they route through. Keep the two separate so the seam gets designed on its own merits rather than emerging from whichever call site was edited first.</notes>
</task>
